    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Tru Grit treadmills come up whenever someone shops for a curved treadmill under $2,500, because the Grit
          Runner is one of very few at that price with heavy resistance settings for sled-push work. Tru Grit
          Fitness is a Southern California equipment company — a 2021 San Diego Business Journal profile placed
          it in Vista — that also sells racks, dumbbells and an air bike. It makes two treadmills: the Grit
          Runner and the more expensive Grit Runner Elite. This guide sets out what the figures are, where the
          sources disagree, what the warranty covers, where to buy and what to compare, including what to make
          of the fact that Tru Grit's own website was not working on the day we checked.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Is a Tru Grit Treadmill Worth Buying? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The standard Grit Runner can be, at $2,000 to $2,500 from a dealer that will handle warranty
            claims: it is one of the cheapest curved treadmills with real sled-push settings. The Grit Runner
            Elite, at $3,495, is harder to recommend over an AssaultRunner Pro or TrueForm Trainer at the same
            price, because Tru Grit does not publish its weight rating.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Tru Grit's treadmill range is two curved manual treadmills, both self-powered, both with a resistance
            system for pushing. That is their appeal: most curved treadmills under $3,000 either have no
            adjustable resistance or have magnetic levels that are not set up for sled work. The Grit Runner's six
            levels are explicitly split into running, walking and sled-push settings.
          </p>
          <p>
            The drawbacks are about information and support rather than the idea. Dealers' listings give few
            figures and occasionally contradict each other, independent reviews are mixed on build quality, and on
            5 November 2026 Tru Grit's own website address was showing a domain-for-sale page. Dealers were still
            listing both treadmills as in stock, and an Amazon listing for the Grit Runner existed, so the products
            are on sale. But you should buy from a dealer that will deal with a warranty claim itself. We have not
            tested any Tru Grit treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-is-tru-grit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Who Is Tru Grit Fitness?</h2>
<p>
            Tru Grit Fitness is a California fitness-equipment brand. A 2021 San Diego Business Journal profile
            describes it as an equipment maker headquartered in Vista, and a local directory places it in nearby
            Carlsbad. Its range, as listed by its dealers, includes the two Grit Runner treadmills, the Reaper
            magnetic-resistance air bike, power racks, a foldable <a href="/best-folding-squat-racks/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>wall-mounted rack</a>, benches, hex dumbbells and
            plate storage — a garage-gym range rather than a home-cardio one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The website problem</h3>
          <p>
            On 5 November 2026, trugritfitness.com — the address dealers and older reviews point to — redirected to
            a page offering the domain for sale. We do not know why, and it does not by itself mean the company has
            stopped trading: six US dealers were listing its treadmills as in stock with listings updated that day.
            Customer reviews on the company's Better Business Bureau profile, seen in search results, include
            complaints about service and say the business is not BBB-accredited; another review says a new team is
            working to improve its reputation. Those are anecdotes, not findings. The practical consequence is the
            same either way: the dealer, not Tru Grit, is the party you can reach.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Not Tru Grit, True or Grit</h3>
          <p>
            Tru Grit is unrelated to <a href="/true-fitness-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>True Fitness</a>, the commercial treadmill maker whose machines appear next to
            it in dealer searches, and to the various gyms called True Grit. If a dealer search for "Tru Grit"
            shows a True Fitness Performance or Stryker treadmill, that is a different company.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Recalls</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database for Tru Grit, Grit and Tru
            Grit Fitness on 5 November 2026 and found no recalls.
          </p>
        </div>

